gpu_decode_2026-09-17_rtx2080.tsv

First sweep of this crate's GPU backend on a hardware GPU. The "before" of the same sweep, with
`gpu/` as it was at 417be423, is gpu_decode_2026-09-17_rtx2080_pretuning.tsv.

adapter: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2080 [Vulkan DiscreteGpu; NVIDIA 580.178.04] (proprietary driver;
  nouveau blacklisted, /dev/nvidia* world-readable, nvidia_uvm loaded, persistence mode on).
  8 GiB. Vulkan instance 1.4.341 (libvulkan1 1.4.341.0-1). Under load `nvidia-smi` reports SM
  1515 MHz and memory 7000 MHz, so the card was not stuck at idle clocks.
wgpu: 30.0.1
host: dev (Ryzen 9 9950X3D, 32 threads, 60 GiB), Ubuntu, kernel 7.0.0-31-generic. Shared with
  other agents.
command: RAYON_NUM_THREADS=8 nice -n 19 gpu_bench --adapter "NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2080"
  --rounds 7 --max-size 4096 --out <this file>
commit: 0120c800 (the pretuning file's gpu/ is 417be423)
columns: header comment of gpu/examples/gpu_bench.rs. `gpu_device_ms` is the sum of the tiles'
  compute-pass durations from GPU timestamp queries; `gpu_wall_ms` adds the latent upload, the
  submit and the readback of three f32 planes. cpu_mt_ms is this crate's CPU engine with
  RAYON_NUM_THREADS=8 (not the box's 32 threads, so it understates a full-box CPU run);
  cpu_1t_ms is one thread. Every number is the median of interleaved rounds.

Crossover, GPU wall against the 8-thread CPU engine, from the `synthesis` rows:
  SOP   between 256x256 (2.64 vs 2.10 ms, CPU wins) and 384x384 (3.08 vs 3.17, GPU wins)
  BOP   between 128x128 (2.73 vs 1.84, CPU) and 256x256 (3.29 vs 4.25, GPU)
  HOP   between 128x128 (10.40 vs 9.92, CPU) and 256x256 (18.90 vs 35.19, GPU)
The heavier the transform, the smaller the picture at which the GPU wins; the GPU's floor is the
25 to 160 dispatches a picture costs however small it is. At 1024x1024 the GPU is 2.1x (SOP),
1.5x (BOP) and 1.3x (HOP) the 8-thread CPU, and 6.0x / 6.0x / 4.3x one thread.

Two caveats, both visible in the file:

* **Sustained clocks and a warm pool.** A size sweep drives the card continuously and reuses one
  workspace across every size, so the later rows run at sustained rather than boost clocks and
  bind pooled buffers sized for the largest picture seen so far. The same 1024x1024 HOP picture
  measures 651.8 ms here and 498.0 ms in gpu_profile_2026-09-17_rtx2080.tsv, where each size gets
  a fresh context; the 560x888 BOP row, which runs last, measures 32.3 ms here against 9.7 ms in
  a fresh context (and 9.4 ms if the same sweep stops at 2048 instead of 4096 — the pool never
  shrinks, see gpu/README.md "Open" item 6). Both are honest; the sweep is what a long-lived
  decoder under load gets, the profile is what one picture costs. Before / after comparisons in
  gpu/README.md use the profile pair, measured back to back under identical conditions.
* **Shared box.** Rows visibly off the curve happen (this run: Bop 1536x1536 at 76.06 ms device
  against 56.01 ms in a repeat of the same binary; an earlier run of the same sweep had a
  non-monotonic cpu_mt column because another agent was running browser tests at 14 cores — that
  run was discarded and this one, whose cpu_mt is monotonic in size for all three operating
  points, kept). Read the curve, not a single row.

Readback, not arithmetic, dominates the largest pictures: SOP 4096x4096 is 138.9 ms of device
time and 226.7 ms of wall, the difference being 201 MB of f32 planes over PCIe. The
`decode_to_gpu` + `to_rgba_texture` path avoids that copy entirely.
